<p>President Donald Trump dismissed reports that the United Kingdom may deploy an aircraft carrier to the Middle East in connection with the war with Iran. He said the United States does not need British help and framed the conflict as essentially decided. His remarks deepened a public clash with UK Prime Minister Keir Starmer, even as the two countries maintain a close military relationship. Coverage links the dispute to wider disagreements about what role the UK should play in the campaign, and what support London is willing to provide for U.S. operations.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/Hb3ZatPSuAt3fZ2JeTjhq2kzgO81Etk5cwxPbAFOQL-FSORHCdA0L14SPtEB3K8AqDzj_EDypwFBZHOC1qHtiKbmeH7zRP0Z1kWduUD0rkAdGyqnNBagFN74dILQLhCLCY3vBBeFQiRr-cspdO1zMnmSeNC6ObYZ9FMA043wy7hl8NLeqrZwpLazdVlxh1rmPx2pQ4Qi1AiXaAP1uGKyZh94eQGWvDu7Yixj0ostvjnwRi24Wl2yqy4jrogYbdDH6fOQjKLgUr98quW1xQ' alt='A U.S. Air Force B-1 Lancer bomber lands at RAF Fairford in Fairford, England, as reporting highlights UK base access for U.S. strikes.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Carrier specifics: Reuters reporting carried by Al-Monitor and The Globe and Mail said Trump claimed Britain was giving “serious thought” to sending two aircraft carriers to the Middle East—an idea he dismissed as unnecessary.</li><li>Rhetoric framing: Le Monde highlighted Trump’s mocking line that the UK was trying to join only after victory—“after we’ve already won!”—in a public message aimed at Starmer.</li><li>Relationship claim: Reuters reporting carried by Al-Monitor said Trump accused Starmer of helping “ruin” the countries’ historically close relationship.</li><li>Operational posture: The Globe and Mail reported Starmer is allowing the U.S. to use British bases for strikes the UK describes as “defensive” against Iranian missile sites.</li></ul><blockquote>We don&#x27;t need people that join wars after we&#x27;ve already won! - President Donald Trump</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>President Donald Trump: He sai